welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  today is Professor Ava's Amana a  professor of history and international  and area studies and director of the  Iranian studies initiative at the  Macmillan Center professor amma knots  teaching and research interests include  modern Iran and the Middle East  she ISM and apocalypticism he has  written several publications including  pivot of the universe and resurrection  and renewal professor Amina is with us  today to talk about his newest book  apocalyptic Islam and Iranian Shiism  welcome professor on the knot thank you  so much I'm glad to be here in your  newest book you explore the history of  Shi Islam what is she Islam well she  Islam is really a historical phenomenon  in terms of its long duration over the  course of Islamic history starting from  the very beginning of Islam based on  some kind of a religious or belief  disagreement in early centuries of Islam  and then gradually developing into  distinct communities from Sunni Islam  particularly from 16th century became  more of a religion of a state in the  case of Iran which is the largest Shi  community in the world with but perhaps  70 million or 60 odd million of the  population also there are communities of  she's in other parts of the Middle East  and Beyond including in southern Iraq  which is the majority of population of  Iraq in southern Lebanon when also there  is a large community in Pakistan in  northern Arabian Peninsula you would see  variety of community  of she's she's and therefore can be  defined both in terms of community and  in terms of beliefs in terms of beliefs  are something which is characteristic of  schism  is this kind of a preoccupation with a  sense of suffering over a course of time  a kind of a story of suffering which is  very much symbolized by the sacred  figures of early Islam and it's also a  history of expectation for some  messianic return and in that regard  indeed she has much in common with other  religious traditions virtually all  religious traditions one might say which  all have an element of mechanism but  more specifically with Christianity and  the idea of the Jesus second coming with  Judaism and the idea of the return of  the Messiah and indeed with  Zoroastrianism the indigenous religion  of Iran where she is played a very  important part in all these traditions  there is this sense of idea of a return  or return of a heroic saintly prophetic  figure sometimes at the end of the time  and it's kind of an closely associated  with the notion of Apocalypse in all  these religions and schism also inherits  or indeed influenced much by all of  these traditions as major similarities  in many ways with Christianity but also  with the other of religious traditions  this is another characteristic important  characteristic of schism  okay so what's the premise of your book  well the the book tends to emphasize  that this messianic apocalyptic aspect  and she's that endured over fourteen  hundred years  has sometimes been overlooked in favor  of him or what might call normative  mainstream Orthodox form of schism which  like stone Islam or like other religions  is very much institutionalized it's  based on the part of the clergy it's  based on a certain set of belief systems  this kind of alternative way of looking  at religion this way of looking at  religion is kind of a desire for a  messianic return has often been less  studied and the purpose of the book  therefore is to try to show over a  course of time how this has developed  how this tendency has developed within  Islam and more specifically within  schism  a couple of chapters of my new book  actually tends to look at it both within  a comparative framework with comparison  to other religions and within Islam  itself and then try to look at its  development over the course of time both  in medieval in early modern times and of  course a big chunk of it in modern times  that is mostly 19th and 20th centuries  ok so what drew you to this topic did he  did it evolve out of your other research  or something else both actually  as you have mentioned in the  introduction I have been working and  reading and writing and teaching aspects  of Iranian history Middle Eastern  history and Islamic history of which the  notion of these kind of movements of  change movements of reform and movements  of revolutionary movements within Islam  many of them with a Shiite component  played a very important part and as such  there has been a kind of a lifetime  interest in the subject and indeed this  book develop  as a result of some studies that I had  done in forms of articles or various  chapters that I had written before and  now I have put them together with some  new material in order to and to my great  surprise to see that they have such a  coherence in the form of a kind of a  single narrative that runs throughout  throughout the book the second point  about it is that contemporary Iran I was  surprised to see that some of the themes  that I have been preoccupied with has a  new life in a sense particularly under  the present president of Iran this kind  of a anticipation for the return of a  messianic figure the math he was going  to bring change into the world and so  forth is very strong and indeed the last  chapter of the book is a extensive study  of various manifestations of the idea of  the return of the idea of this kind of a  messianic manifestation in today's Iran  so let's talk a little bit about the  research you needed to do for the book  well the research was in part based on  textual material in pre-modern times  focusing mostly on the writings of the  many of the figures of this kind of  messianic inspiration many of the trends  in schism that led eventually to the  emergence of new religious forms or even  new religions such as for instance the  famous Bobby Baha'i religion that I  indeed there is a chapter in this book  about how it initially was transformed  from the kind of a body of Shi messianic  ideas into a new religious belief and  then of course in more modern times I  deal  on variety of other sources some of it  newspapers travel accounts histories  personal narratives perhaps less of  archival work because this is a kind of  a topic that does not lend itself well  to archival studies much of what I have  studied this in this book indeed was an  undercurrent which were most of the time  was either not seen easily by The  Outsiders by the people observers and  mostly it's an attempt to try to read  against the grain of what the historical  text would tell you so what is  apocalypticism and how has it influenced  the development of modern GI theology as  I was saying apocalypticism in a very  broad sense of course it has a very  specific Christian biblical meaning in  the sense of its association with the  Book of Apocalypse of Revelation but the  term itself in a kind of a technical  sense means something broader than that  means is an aspiration for a for a an  end to your notion of present time to  the human society's notion of present  time sometimes it's very violent as it's  often the term associates with the kind  of a catastrophic cataclysmic event  let's say in Hollywood movies and  sometimes it is more specifically in a  symbolic way defined as a kind of a  symbolic shift in time  therefore the end of an era an end of an  epoch and the beginning of a new epoch  is what it's indeed desired by this  collective change usually the  apocalyptic change is a component of a  messianic return you have a charismatic  prophetic saintly figure which has been  expected has been promised for a long  time who emerges appears in a bodily  human form and indeed in many ways it  reflects the aspirations of the society  in which he comes from or she comes from  and then it brings about usually in a  dramatic way usually even by declaring  that the end of the time has arrived and  the beginning of a new time a kind of a  utopic view a utopian view of the world  in which the realm of justice and equity  has to be benefited by the entire human  race sometimes very specific societies  communities sometimes web in a broader  sense and in a sense there is a there is  an idealism associated with it sometimes  it's very religious as indeed  apocalypticism often implies in terms of  the religions that I have named  sometimes it could be purely secular we  can see in the writings of many  historians  I'm sorry many religious figures and  some literary works even in poetry we  can see some this apocalyptic  aspirations as I said a little cinema we  can find that phenomenon so it's  something that seems to have been very  deeply engrained with human nature to  try to see a moment of shift from one  pattern of life to another okay so what  doing the research did you find most  surprising well and perhaps the most  surprising for me was the emergence  basically of  these ideas which to us as historians or  scholars seem to be purely a historical  phenomenon that you would not expect in  a modern or rather postmodern societies  of today to remarriage and I was very  surprised to see in a sense despite all  years of following these trends in  today's Iran to try to see that they are  reemerging not only with most recent  episode but indeed you can see look at  the Iranian Islamic Revolution of 1979  as I have argued in my book as indeed a  very much a messianic phenomenon with  Ayatollah Khomeini the leader of the  Revolution is really kind of most  prophetic figure that in many ways  manifests or reflects this kind of a  characteristic of a messianic figure and  then of course later on in a more  explicit way in the recent past five six  years under President Ahmadinejad so the  fact that you can see indeed a present  updated version of the emergence of  these themes in today's Shiism or  today's Islam as a matter of fact was  was a surprise to me and in a sense  confirmation of what I have been saying  in my other work earlier on so do you  have a sense of why that is happening  sure as I was pointing out apocalyptic  or messianic aspirations is a very  complex phenomenon and it can be  attributed in part to the fact that we  are at the end of the third decade of an  Islamic Revolution speaking specifically  in the case of Iran and there is a sense  of disillusionment with what the  revolution had promised and never  provided there was  this creates enthusiasm in the early  years of the revolution the years of the  first decade basically under Ayatollah  Khomeini himself that he would bring an  ideal Islamic society into this world  then there was a very traumatic period  of eight years of war with Iraq which in  a sense by itself was very apocalyptic  in its nature like the first world war  which many historians would indeed claim  as spite that created the sense of  disillusionment the sense of end the  sense of total break from the norms of  the society and in this case of the  iran-iraq war we can find the same  phenomenon and then after that for about  10-15 years a a promise for the  emergence of a more open society  particularly on the president heart I  mean there was this great promise that  the Islamic Revolution is eventually  kind going to be to accept the world  outside and try to come to terms with  the war the more reformist vision of  revolution and that also failed in a  sense or never really managed to  transform the Iranian society and  therefore the third phase is what we are  witnessing today which tends to promise  going back again to the expectations for  a better society expectations for coming  of a figure that is going to bring about  change and eventually materialize what  has been promised so one can in a few  words can in nutshell can regard this as  a as a reflection of the trauma of  disillusionment of the Islamic  Revolution fascinating thank you very  much for being here with us today and  sharing some  your work thank you for more information  about Professor Amana and his newest  book please visit our website at Yale  edu backslash Macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale  you
 

